{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Daniel jolted awake to the blinding white of his bedroom ceiling, his heart hammering against his ribs. The air smelled sterile, like plastic and ozone, and when he flexed his fingers, the nails clicked like brittle chipboard. He sat up, only to freeze—his reflection in the dresser mirror wasn’t his. The man staring back wore a pixelated grunge flannel, his stubble jagged like broken glass, and when he touched the surface, his fingers sank into a shimmering, flickering void. A UI panel flickered at the edge of his vision: *\"Health: 100/100 • Ammo: 0/5 • Objective: Survive.\"* Outside his window, the city skyline loomed, but the skyscrapers pulsed with an unnatural glow, their windows displaying distorted game-like graphs and numbers. His phone buzzed on the nightstand—no signal. When he grabbed it, the device crumbled into static in his palm, leaving only a single, looping message on the cracked screen: *\"You are Player 1.\"*\n\nHe bolted for the door, his sneakers slapping against a floor that vibrated with an eerie synth rhythm beneath his feet. The hallway stretched impossibly long, the walls lined with flickering neon signs that read *\"RESPAWN POINT +100M\"* and *\"BOSS: THE HOURGLASS.\"* Voices echoed from the stairwell—players, or NPCs?—laughing, arguing, some screaming in terror. A health pack lay abandoned in the corner, its glow pulsing like a dying heartbeat. Daniel ducked into an alley, where the air hummed with invisible code. A rat skittered past, its fur made of shifting pixels. He clenched his fists, suddenly remembering the weight of his old controller—*the right stick stickier from decades of use.* If this was a game, then someone was playing him. And the rules? He had no idea what they were yet. But he knew one thing for certain: the clock at the top of the screen was counting down, and he wasn’t sure if it was seconds o